Define and declare Tcl 6.7's variadic functions with <stdarg.h> and ANSI
prototypes (docs/M2-REPORT.md, section 5; M1's patches/0002 for Tiny
Tcl, which shares this code).

Tcl 6.7 defines Tcl_AppendResult, Tcl_SetErrorCode and Tcl_VarEval with
<varargs.h> (included by tclInt.h), and tcl.h declares them as "f()" when
compiling C, so callers see no prototype. Open Watcom passes the
arguments of an unprototyped call in registers but reads those of a "..."
definition from the stack, so every error message corrupted memory: the
port's first error, "catch {incr x y}", wrote into the ROM BIOS under
DOSBox-X and lost the script's output. Borland's default stack
convention hid this. panic(), a K&R function of nine fixed arguments
called with fewer, is made a vfprintf-based "..." function for the same
reason, and tclHash.c's "extern void panic();" is removed in favour of
tclInt.h's prototype.

diff -ru a/panic.c b/panic.c
--- a/panic.c
+++ b/panic.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <stdarg.h>
 
 /*
  *----------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -38,17 +39,15 @@
  *----------------------------------------------------------------------
  */
 
-	/* VARARGS ARGSUSED */
 void
-panic(format, arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5, arg6, arg7, arg8)
-    char *format;		/* Format string, suitable for passing to
-				 * fprintf. */
-    char *arg1, *arg2, *arg3;	/* Additional arguments (variable in number)
-				 * to pass to fprintf. */
-    char *arg4, *arg5, *arg6, *arg7, *arg8;
+panic(char *format, ...)	/* Format string, suitable for passing to
+				 * fprintf, then its arguments. */
 {
-    (void) fprintf(stderr, format, arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5, arg6,
-	    arg7, arg8);
+    va_list argList;
+
+    va_start(argList, format);
+    (void) vfprintf(stderr, format, argList);
+    va_end(argList);
     (void) fflush(stderr);
     abort();
 }
